== Learning Objectives

By the end of the lesson, students will be able to:

+ Name and describe the four main stages of the water cycle: evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection.
+ Explain that the sun's energy powers the water cycle.
+ Label a water cycle diagram and use it to explain how water moves.

== Key Vocabulary

#table(
  columns: (auto, 1fr),
  inset: 6pt,
  [*Word*], [*Kid-friendly meaning*],
  [Evaporation], [Heat from the sun turns liquid water into an invisible gas called water vapor.],
  [Condensation], [Water vapor cools and turns back into tiny drops of liquid water, forming clouds.],
  [Precipitation], [Water falling from clouds as rain, snow, sleet, or hail.],
  [Collection], [Water gathering in oceans, lakes, rivers, and underground.],
  [Water vapor], [Water in the form of a gas.],
)

== Materials

- Whiteboard or chart paper and markers
- Large water cycle poster or projected diagram
- Teacher demo: a clear jar, hot tap water, a small plate or lid, and ice cubes
- Per pair: one zip-top plastic bag, water, blue food coloring, permanent marker, tape
- Student handout: blank water cycle diagram with a word bank
- Exit ticket slips and pencils

== Preparation (before class)

- Make one sample "water cycle in a bag" ahead of time (about 50 mL of blue water, sun and clouds drawn on the outside, taped to a sunny window) so students can see real results.
- Fill the demo jar with hot water just before the demo.
- Prepare bags and supplies in a tray for each pair.

